---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import sweden from "../assets/sweden.png";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
	<div class="p-4 flex items-center justify-center">
		<h1 class="text-4xl bg-ctp-mantle p-8 rounded-2xl">Burnout</h1>
	</div>
	<div class="flex flex-row flex-1 items-center justify-center flex-wrap gap-16 bg-ctp-mantle m-8 p-8 rounded-2xl">
		<div class="w-lg h-auto">
			<Image
				class="object-contain inline"
				src={sweden}
				alt="Photo by Nataliya Vaitkevich from Pexels: https://www.pexels.com/photo/overworked-employee-lying-in-front-of-laptop-6837643/"
			/>
		</div>
		<p class="text-xl">
			Many people would assume that shortening the workday would mean a decrease in productivity per worker, and that the best way to increase productivity
			would be to lengthen the workday. They might also assume that a lower number of hours worked would decrease revenue and profit. These assumptions are
			incorrect, however. In Sweden, they used to stick to an 8 hour workday, similar to the current standard in America. However, due to workers being
			overworked, and coming home exhausted, they began looking into other options. One of which was a shift from that 8 hour workday to a 6 hour workday,
			keeping the same wages for the workers. What started out as an experiment initially was extremely successful, and actually increased productivity in many
			areas. The workers get home earlier, and with enough time and energy to relax after work (Efficiency Up). While this example may be over a decade old, it
			shows that simply lowering the number of hours worked doesn’t lower productivity, at least not to the same degree. The example from Sweden covers a wide
			range of jobs, both in nursing homes and factories, showing that the benefit isn’t limited to one field or job. The companies that stuck to this schedule
			also had better retention and more applicants, even when compared to better paying jobs (Efficiency Up). While the increase in productivity itself
			provides plenty of draw towards the shorter schedule, the desire of the workers to work fewer hours than to receive better pay goes to show how impactful
			this change of schedule can truly be. Those fewer hours could give workers with kids more time to spend with them, or give them more time to care for
			their own health Of those interviewed, the workers expressed great appreciation for the shorter days, and brought up how it allows for much more of a
			work-life balance than the traditional 8 hour schedule (Efficiency Up). Even if productivity were to go down, although it’s shown that it wouldn’t, the
			benefits in the personal lives of the workers greatly outweigh the potential minor loss in productivity when anything other than money is taken into
			consideration. Unfortunately, given the massive changes stemming from a shift in workday length, there aren’t many other studies showing the effects of
			this change.
		</p>
	</div>
	<div class="flex items-center justify-center">
		<a href="/" class="underline text-2xl bg-ctp-mantle p-4 rounded-2xl">back</a>
	</div>
	<div class="flex items-center justify-center">
		<a href="/burnout" class="underline text-2xl bg-ctp-mantle p-4 rounded-2xl">next</a>
	</div>
</Layout>
